# xpf (#1917 §6.3c): keep needrestart from auto-restarting xpfd.
#
# Ubuntu server installs needrestart by default; at the end of an apt
# transaction it scans for processes running deleted binaries and
# auto-restarts their services. That would cut the dataplane mid-apt
# even though our packaging never stops xpfd on upgrade. Two defences:
#
#   1. The running xpfd binary lives under the non-dpkg runtime path
#      (the live /usr/local/sbin symlink chain), which an `apt upgrade
#      xpf` only refreshes in the dpkg-static staging area — so the
#      running binary is not deleted and needrestart has nothing to
#      trigger on for the common case.
#   2. Belt-and-braces: explicitly blacklist xpfd.service so even if a
#      future layout exposes a deleted-binary condition, needrestart
#      never restarts the dataplane. The verified cut-over is driven
#      explicitly by the in-place upgrade mechanism, never by
#      needrestart.
$nrconf{override_rc}{qr(^xpfd\.service$)} = 0;
$nrconf{override_rc}{qr(^xpf-day0-config\.service$)} = 0;
